!% -~S
!% $OMIT_UNUSED_ROUTINES=1

! ============================================================================
!  TWO ROOMS  —  Frotzsmith sample (PunyInform)
! ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  The PunyInform port of the "Two Rooms" demo: two rooms, a takeable lamp,
!  and movement. PunyInform is a compact replacement library aimed at small,
!  fast Z-machine games. Compiler options are set on the `!%` lines above.
!  Try:  look, take lamp, examine lamp, north, south, inventory
!
!  Lines starting with `!` (below the first two) are comments: the compiler
!  ignores everything from `!` to the end of the line. The Standard Library
!  version of this game is demo.inf; comparing the two shows exactly what
!  changes between the libraries (very little, in a game this small).
! ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  HOW THIS FILE IS STRUCTURED
!  ────────────────────────────
!  1. `!%` compiler-option lines  (read by the compiler before compiling)
!  2. Game identity Constants
!  3. INITIAL_LOCATION_VALUE      (tells PunyInform where the player starts)
!  4. Include "globals.h"         (PunyInform global vars + configuration)
!  5. Include "puny.h"            (parser + verbs + grammar — all in one file)
!  6. Room and object definitions (after the includes)
!  7. [ Initialise; ]             (required; here it only prints a welcome)
!
!  `!%` COMPILER OPTION LINES — keep them as the very first lines
!  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
!  A `!%` line at the top of the file is a compiler switch, as if typed on
!  the compiler's command line; lower down it would only be a comment.
!  !% -~S          turns strict mode off. Strict mode (normally on) adds
!                  run-time checks that catch programming mistakes, at the
!                  cost of a larger, slower story. PunyInform's template
!                  turns it off to keep games small; it is recommended, not
!                  required (the game compiles and plays without it).
!
!  !% $OMIT_UNUSED_ROUTINES=1
!                  instructs the Inform 6 compiler to remove any routine
!                  that is never called. This can save several KB in the
!                  output .z3/.z5 file — important for Z-machine size limits.
!
!  PUNYINFORM vs STANDARD LIBRARY — key differences
!  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
!  Feature                  Standard Library        PunyInform
!  ─────────────────────── ─────────────────────── ────────────────────────
!  Library files            Parser + VerbLib +      globals.h + puny.h
!                           Grammar (3 includes)    (2 includes)
!  Start location           location = X in         Constant
!                           Initialise              INITIAL_LOCATION_VALUE=X
!  Story formats            z5 / z8 (and Glulx)     z3 / z5 / z8
!  Strict mode              usually left on         usually switched off (-~S)
!  Target audience          full-featured games     compact / retro games
!  Exits line               Frotzsmith's Exits.h    none (no Exits.h)
!  Room text on a revisit   repeated (VERBOSE is     name only (BRIEF is the
!                           the 6.12 default)        default; VERBOSE changes it)
!  Stock replies (wording)  "You can't see any      "Sorry, I don't understand
!                           such thing." for an      what "door" means." for a
!                           unknown word             word the game doesn't know
!
!  HOW THE GAME FLOWS
!    You start in the Cottage, where a brass lamp lies. TAKE LAMP (and EXAMINE
!    it), go NORTH to the Meadow and SOUTH back; INVENTORY shows the lamp.
!    No puzzle and no ending: a map with one thing in it.
!
!  MAP
!        Meadow
!          |
!        Cottage   <- start; the brass lamp is here
! ============================================================================

! --- Game identity constants -------------------------------------------------
!  `Constant Story` supplies the game title. The Standard Library and
!  PunyInform both print it in bold in the opening banner. (The status bar
!  shows the current room and the score/moves.)
!
!  `Constant Headline` is the subtitle line in the opening banner.
!  `^` inside a string literal is the Inform 6 newline escape character.

Constant Story "TWO ROOMS";
Constant Headline "^A Frotzsmith sample (PunyInform edition).^";

! --- Starting location (PunyInform-specific) --------------------------------
!  INITIAL_LOCATION_VALUE is read by globals.h: it becomes the starting
!  value of the global `location`, and after Initialise the library moves
!  the player there. This replaces the Standard
!  Library pattern of writing `location = Cottage;` inside [ Initialise; ].
!  The value must be the Inform 6 identifier of a room object defined later
!  in this file (forward references are fine; the compiler resolves them).
!  It has to come before Include "globals.h", which is where it is read.

Constant INITIAL_LOCATION_VALUE = Cottage;

! --- PunyInform library includes --------------------------------------------
!  globals.h must be included FIRST. It declares the global variables used
!  throughout PunyInform (location, score, turns, the_time, etc.), the
!  attributes and properties objects use, and reads
!  any configuration constants you set before this line (including
!  INITIAL_LOCATION_VALUE and optional feature-flags like OPTIONAL_EXTENDED_METAVERBS).
!  `Include "file";` reads the file at this point, as if pasted in.

Include "globals.h";

!  puny.h is the monolithic PunyInform library. It contains:
!    — the parser (tokenises input, resolves noun phrases)
!    — all built-in verb handlers (Take, Drop, Examine, Look, Go, …)
!    — the grammar tables (maps words like "take"/"get" to the Take action)
!  Unlike the Standard Library there is NO separate `Include "Grammar";` at
!  the end of the file. puny.h includes PunyInform's grammar.h itself.
!
!  Place all Object, Class, and custom Verb definitions after puny.h, as is
!  done here. (An `Extend` of a library verb must come after it.)

Include "puny.h";

! --- Room: Cottage ----------------------------------------------------------
!  `Object Cottage "Cottage"` — `Cottage` is the Inform 6 identifier used in
!  code. "Cottage" (in quotes) is the player-visible short name.
!  After the header, `with` starts the PROPERTIES (named values, separated by
!  commas) and `has` the ATTRIBUTES (on/off flags). The `;` ends the object.
!
!  `with description "…"` — long description shown on >LOOK or first entry.
!  (PunyInform starts in BRIEF mode: come back SOUTH from the Meadow and you
!  get just the room name and its contents. The 6.12 Standard Library starts
!  in VERBOSE mode and repeats the description every time.)
!
!  `n_to Meadow` — the n_to *property* makes NORTH move the player to the
!  Meadow room object. Directions available: n_to, s_to, e_to, w_to, ne_to,
!  nw_to, se_to, sw_to, u_to (up), d_to (down), in_to, out_to.
!  A direction property can hold a room object (simple link), a string (a
!  message printed instead of moving), or a routine (computed or
!  conditional exit). A direction with no property gets "You can't go that
!  way."
!  (`Meadow` is declared further down; that is allowed.)
!
!  The "sturdy door" is only words in the description, not an object:
!  EXAMINE DOOR gets PunyInform's "Sorry, I don't understand what "door"
!  means." because the game has no such dictionary word at all.
!
!  `has light` — the `light` attribute marks the room as lit. Every room
!  the player visits needs a light source: either `has light` on the room
!  itself, or a carried object that has the `light` attribute.

Object Cottage "Cottage"
  with description
        "A cosy one-room cottage. A sturdy door leads north into daylight.",
       n_to Meadow,
  has  light;

! --- Object: lamp (child of Cottage) ----------------------------------------
!  `Object ->` places this object as an immediate child of the previously
!  defined room (Cottage) at compile time. When the game starts the lamp
!  is lying on the floor of the Cottage, visible and takeable.
!
!  One arrow  (->)   = placed inside the preceding Object/room
!  Two arrows (-> ->) = placed inside the preceding child object (nested)
!
!  `with name 'brass' 'lamp'` — the `name` property lists dictionary words
!  the parser matches when the player types noun phrases. Words are in single
!  quotes. Both 'brass' and 'lamp' are added, so "take lamp", "take brass",
!  and "examine brass lamp" all refer to this object.
!  Single quotes = words the player may TYPE; double quotes = text the game
!  PRINTS. The short name "brass lamp" is never used for parsing.
!
!  `description "…"` — response to >EXAMINE LAMP. Objects without a
!  description get the library's stock reply instead.
!
!  No `has` line is needed for a plain portable object. The library lets the
!  player TAKE any object that lacks `static` or `scenery`. To make the lamp
!  actually illuminate dark rooms, add `has light` to this object.

Object -> lamp "brass lamp"
  with name 'brass' 'lamp',
       description "A small brass lamp, the kind every adventurer carries.";

! --- Room: Meadow -----------------------------------------------------------
!  The second room. `s_to Cottage` completes the bidirectional link:
!    Cottage → (north) → Meadow
!    Meadow  → (south) → Cottage
!  Each room independently lists its own exits; there is no automatic
!  reverse-link — you must add both sides by hand.
!  No arrow: Meadow is a top-level object (a room), not inside the lamp.

Object Meadow "Meadow"
  with description
        "A sunlit meadow dotted with wildflowers. The cottage lies to the south.",
       s_to Cottage,
  has  light;

! --- Initialise routine -----------------------------------------------------
!  A routine is written `[ Name; statements ];`. PunyInform calls
!  Initialise once at startup, before the banner and before it moves the
!  player into INITIAL_LOCATION_VALUE. The routine must exist (the library
!  calls it by name), but in PunyInform it does not have to set the
!  location. Its typical use is printing a welcome message or running
!  one-time setup logic before the first prompt.
!
!  `print "…^^"` — prints the string; `^` = newline, so `^^` = blank line.
!  The two separate print calls let you edit each line independently without
!  touching the other.
!
!  A bare string literal used as a statement (the `"…"` shorthand for
!  print + newline + rtrue) works exactly the same in PunyInform as in the
!  Standard Library — it is part of the Inform 6 language, not the library.
!  `print` is shown here for clarity. (Only a return value of 2 would change
!  anything: it hides the banner. This routine returns 0.)

[ Initialise;
    print "^Welcome to the Frotzsmith demo (PunyInform edition)!^^";
    print "Try: look, take lamp, examine lamp, north, south, inventory.^";
];
